{
  "items": [
    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 1, "reason": "The headline, the decision block and claim quality show that nothing can be picked, explain that the latency budget applies per task, and name the risk that Brisk-S high + retry clears multi-hop by only 0.3 points. The lede then says that reading latency per task 'would show whether one configuration ... can meet both gates'. The page's own confidence line says neither configuration that clears 97% everywhere can be rescued. options_dominance draws the same conclusion: an overall p95 above 600 ms (870 ms and 2551 ms) puts at least one task over the budget. So a reader of the first screen is told a single-configuration answer is still open when it is not. Only a split of tasks is still open."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 1, "reason": "Each claim matches its values table. Claim quality's rows show only Atlas-M high + retry and Brisk-S high + retry at 97% or above in all four tasks, and every other configuration misses on multi-hop. Claim pair shows -0.7 points, interval -1.7 to +0.4, as the claim says. The inconsistency is page-level. The confidence clause says neither configuration that clears pass rate everywhere can be rescued. The lede, the latency claim ('every configuration is undecided or out') and next step 1 ('whether any single configuration ... can meet the latency budget') treat that as open."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 1, "reason": "The thin 0.3-point clearance on multi-hop is in Watch, and the 37.4% figure is shown under its labelled assumption (it matches settle_cases at 300 assumed cases). The weak spots are elsewhere. Claim pair and the decision block's 'What we know despite it' compare costs (0.18 times as much; $2.84 less) without saying who served each model. Claim levers puts first that low-to-high reasoning with retry 'is not separated overall' (+0.8). It does not say that on multi-hop the same move takes Brisk-S from 95.6% to 97.3%, from missing the gate to clearing it, which is the gap that matters for the decision."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "Nothing omitted changes the call. options_dominance agrees that the left-out Atlas-M and Brisk-S configurations, and every Nano-XS configuration, fail pass rate in at least one task. The model effects on Atlas-M and Nano-XS point the same way as on Brisk-S. The snapshot does state a monthly volume (4,000,000), but the page's reason holds: it does not say whether that counts calls or tasks, and cost does not decide this call."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 1, "reason": "Four confounds are carried into the story and the next steps: latency read only over all tasks, unstated task sizes, best-of-eighteen selection and no control. The serving provider is handled only in Method. The claims that rank costs do not carry it, so a reader of claim pair takes the 0.18x ratio as a property of the model."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names an action and what it would settle: reading per-task p95 and task case counts; declaring whether tasks may be split and which latency line multi-hop is held to; and a multi-hop fix or second retry on Brisk-S low. Step 1 partly asks a question the page's own inference already answers (whether a single configuration can pass), but it is still the step that decides the split."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 1, "reason": "Claims quality, latency, pair and levers each show their own figures and the gap they describe. Claim failures says Brisk-S low + retry 'misses on multi-hop by 1.4 points and on ambiguous entities by 0.6', but its block holds only three example failures, so neither shortfall is drawn. It also carries no caveat for the unstated task sizes, although a 0.6-point miss could fall either side under any plausible task size. In claim levers, the high-reasoning retry row prints its lower bound as '+0.03535', which is unreadable next to the other one-decimal bounds."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "must_fix", "finding": "The lede says reading latency per task 'would show whether one configuration, or a split of tasks between two, can meet both gates'. The page's own confidence clause, and code's inferred fact on Atlas-M high + retry, say otherwise. If each case belongs to one task, an overall p95 of 870 ms (Brisk-S high + retry) or 2551 ms (Atlas-M high + retry) puts at least one task over 600 ms. Every other configuration already misses pass rate in some task. So no single configuration meets both gates; only a split of tasks is open. Restate the headline and lede that way, with the condition named (cases split across tasks). Bring the latency claim ('undecided or out') and next step 1 into line: drop 'whether any single configuration ... can meet the latency budget', and drop Atlas-M high + retry from the per-task read unless it is wanted for a split."},
    {"claim": "pair", "severity": "must_fix", "finding": "Claim pair ('costs 0.18 times as much per thousand calls') and the decision block ('costs $2.84 less per thousand calls ... than Atlas-M high + retry') rank Brisk-S against Atlas-M on cost without naming who served either model. The snapshot does not state the provider or pricing for either arm. Method mentions this, but the claims do not. Name the serving provider for each model in both places, or say there that the provider routing is unstated and the ratio holds only for this run's serving."},
    {"claim": "levers", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Claim levers puts first that low-to-high reasoning with retry is 'not separated overall (+0.8 points)' but costs 357 ms of p95. That reads as if high reasoning buys little. On multi-hop, the same move takes Brisk-S from 95.6% (misses the gate) to 97.3% (clears it), and that is what the call turns on. Add the multi-hop difference (a point estimate, no interval, since task sizes are unstated) to the claim or its block. Also print the high-reasoning retry row's lower bound as +0.04, not +0.03535."},
    {"claim": "failures", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Claim failures says Brisk-S low + retry misses multi-hop by 1.4 points and ambiguous entities by 0.6, but its block draws neither figure, only three examples. Attach the 'Task sizes unstated' caveat to this claim: at the traffic-share size of about 180 ambiguous-entity cases, a 0.6-point miss is well inside a rerun's spread. Either add the two per-task pass rates against the 97% line to the block, or make the sentence about the examples alone."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The confidence clause on unstated task sizes names three near-line cells: Brisk-S high + retry on multi-hop, Atlas-M high without retry on multi-hop at 96.6%, and Brisk-S low + retry on date math at 97%. It leaves out others within about a point that bear on a split: Brisk-S low + retry on ambiguous entities (96.4%), Atlas-M low + retry on multi-hop (96.4%) and Brisk-S high without retry on date math (96.6%). List them, or say the three are examples and not the full set."}
  ]
}
```"}]}

